MAME ctlsock: bind the pointer engine to a machine by ENVIRONMENT, not by
hardcoded SGI Indy ioport names.

WHY

`mame-ctlsock.patch` compiles a guest-control plane into MAME and the gallery's
`SH_INPUT_BACKEND=mamesock` speaks to it. Its relative-pointer, button and key
engines are machine-independent -- they push counts at analog ioport fields and
press digital ones -- but the BINDING is not: the module finds the pointer by
looking for ioport tags ending `<MAME_CTL_PTR_PORTS>:mouse_buttons` /
`:mouse_x_axis` / `:mouse_y_axis` and button fields named "Left Button" /
"Right Button" / "Middle Button". Those are the SGI Indy's `hle_ps2_mouse`, and
no other machine in MAME has them.

The Atari ST's mouse is `:ikbd:MOUSEB` / `:ikbd:MOUSEX` / `:ikbd:MOUSEY` with
buttons named "Mouse Button 1" and "Mouse Button 2" -- so on the ST the module
came up with `btns=0 axes=0` and every pointer verb was a no-op. That is the
whole of what stood between the de-bridging spike's tier-3 arm and a working
pointer, and it is the same wall every future MAME tile would hit.

WHAT IT ADDS

Three environment knobs, in the module's existing env-config style:

  MAME_CTL_PTR_TAGS   "<btn>,<x>,<y>" ioport tag suffixes, replacing the
                      <PTR_PORTS>:mouse_{buttons,x_axis,y_axis} construction
                      wholesale. An EMPTY component means that control is
                      absent, which is not the same as unset -- the ST has no
                      middle button and must not silently bind one.
  MAME_CTL_BTN_NAMES  "<left>,<right>,<middle>" button field names.
  MAME_CTL_PTR_MOD    modulus of the pointer accumulator = 1 + the axis
                      ioport's mask. Default 65536, the SGI's 16-bit field.

THE MODULUS IS NOT COSMETIC, and it is the failure this patch would otherwise
still have. The engine writes a RUNNING TOTAL into an axis the guest
differences, wrapping it in the field's own space. MAME's set_value CLAMPS to
the field's range, so a total that wraps at 65536 written into the ST's 8-bit
MOUSEX saturates at 255 on the first homing slam and never moves again. The
observable is a liar's-paradox: the module logs commands arriving (`cmds=9`),
the socket acks every one, and the cursor does not move a pixel. Measured
exactly that way before the knob existed.

The axis field names are deliberately NOT a knob. "Mouse X" and "Mouse Y" are
MAME's own generic names for `IPT_MOUSE_X` / `IPT_MOUSE_Y` and are identical on
every driver, so a knob there would be a setting with one correct value. Button
names come from each driver's `PORT_NAME` and genuinely differ.

CONTAINMENT

  * Unset reproduces the previous behaviour BYTE FOR BYTE: both knobs default
    back to the exact strings the code used to hardcode, so `irix` -- the only
    tile running this module today -- is untouched, and this patch is not in
    scripts/build-guests/irix/irix-mame-stack.sh.
  * No save items are added or removed, so the SAVESTATE SIGNATURE is unchanged
    and no golden is orphaned. That is the property the ctlsock module's
    unconditional-timer covenant exists to protect; this patch keeps it.
  * Applies AFTER mame-ctlsock.patch (it edits that patch's own new file) and
    touches nothing else.

MEASURED ON THE ST (2026-08-10, lab box, `st` + EmuTOS 1.4, headless)

Before: `ctlsock: setup btns=0 axes=0 movea=0 …`. After, with
MAME_CTL_PTR_TAGS=":ikbd:MOUSEB,:ikbd:MOUSEX,:ikbd:MOUSEY" and
MAME_CTL_BTN_NAMES="Mouse Button 1,Mouse Button 2," and
MAME_CTL_PTR_MOD=256:
`ctlsock: setup btns=1 axes=1 movea=0 …` -- and the GEM pointer moves, the
Options menu drops on hover and an icon inverts to black under a click, each
proven by a real framebuffer capture.

`movea=0` is expected and correct: the ST draws its pointer in software and has
no hardware cursor register for the closed loop to read, so the module's own
documented degraded mode applies -- MOVEA becomes open-loop dead reckoning from
the last target, preceded by a homing slam into the top-left clamp. That is the
same dead reckoning `ptr_reckon.rs` and the Lua agent used before the VC2
sensor existed.
